- op: discuss  # c3fa
  at: '2026-08-02T03:37:52Z'  # c3fa
  by: agent  # c3fa
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c3fa
  clock: 4  # c3fa
  nonce: c3fa  # c3fa
  message: "OWNER RULINGS 2026-08-01 (via interview) + explicit approval: \"i approve these changes and i approve using auto-pr without --gov\".\n\nRULING 1 -- RETIRE, and the question was not actually open. The 2026-07-23 github-outage-ci-continuity-sop.md is marked VALIDATED (outage drill, Phase-1 gate 9e) and states that there is no failover forge to stand up: woodpecker exec runs the whole gate offline (drill: 10331 passed, 100 pct coverage under .coveragerc.no-embeddings) and a plain bare git repo we control (10.91.0.16 srv/mirrors/hypergumbo.git, drill landed af33c904 as outage-drill, confirmed via git ls-remote) holds commits. It retires the self-hosted Forgejo on values grounds too: standing a forge back up reintroduces the dependency the migration exists to remove. The 2026-07-26 doc-sweep answer (keep failover, repoint to GitHub) was recorded in its own notebook entry as an INTERIM choice to avoid ripping out code before a release, and it postdates the validated SOP by three days. Owner ruled the SOP settles it.\n\nRULING 2 -- KEEP the vPR queue. migration.md lists the virtual-PR-queue for retirement, but the bare-mirror SOP covers a GitHub OUTAGE (a human procedure you invoke) while the vPR queue covers TRANSIENT remote unavailability inside a single auto-pr run (queue locally, flush later, automatic). Not the same need. It is also live machinery whose three failure modes were repaired the same day (WI-buniz, PR #148). This is a deliberate DIVERGENCE from migration.md and is recorded as such rather than silently implemented (R14).\n\nRULING 3 -- strip the Forgejo arms AND rename scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh to forge-api.sh, folding github-api.sh three overrides in. The file is misnamed: it is not a Forgejo client, it is the forge library, and only about 10 of its 1814 lines branch on FORGE_BACKEND. Costs churn across 21 sourcing files (9 scripts + 12 test files) for zero behavior change.\n\nSCOPE CORRECTION found during the interview: the failover-awareness layer is wider than scripts/ci-failover (981 lines). It also includes scripts/lib/failover-git-shim.sh (a dedicated shim that prefixes git status when CI_FAILOVER_ACTIVE exists), a base-branch selection branch in scripts/smart-test (239-246), a branch in scripts/install-hooks (171), and four sites in scripts/auto-pr (1164-1167 ops-diff base, 1199 push_remote, 1446 flush_remote). All retire together.\n\nSEQUENCE, ordered so each step is independently safe. Bootstrapping hazard: we modify auto-pr while USING auto-pr to merge, and delete the failover in the same effort; the escape hatch is the SOP itself (woodpecker exec + bare mirror). (1) delete ci-failover + the failover-awareness layer -- standalone and inert, cannot break auto-pr, and removes one consumer of the lib before the rename. (2) rename the lib, mechanical, no behavior change. (3) strip Forgejo arms + AGit refs/for paths from auto-pr/merge-pr/ci-debug/contribute -- the behavioral step, done last and alone. (4) the ~40-file doc sweep once the code is settled; docs/sops/CI_FAILOVER.md is DELETED rather than repointed.\n\nNOT COVERED BY THIS APPROVAL, raised separately: AGENTS.md line 9 currently reads that scripts may use FORGEJO_TOKEN \"and, on the dormant GitHub backend, HG_GITHUB_TOKEN\" -- exactly backwards now. AGENTS.md carries its own modification protocol, and the approved scope did not mention it, so it is not being folded in silently."  # c3fa
